(project cs-interpreters
 (doc "Course: interpreters and code as data — build a Lisp, then fork, diff, and merge programs as trees (CS 201 + CS 202 of archive/proposals/cs-curriculum.md)")
 (status implemented)
 (* agent note (|2026-08-31 authored per borge/habitat-book.borg
     course-documents: a course is a COMPOSITION — this root selects
     chapters in course order for `borge book print/export --root`
     (the per-course paper), and the habitat mirrors it as the notes
     outline doc `course/cs-interpreters` (kind course), rendered by
     libs/book at /book/course/cs-interpreters. Course roots are
     excluded from the canonical book walk (borge composition-root
     rule): they compose chapters, never copy or reorder them.|) *)
 (convention ocaml-dune)
 (section front-matter
  (doc "Who this course is for, what you will be able to do, and how the readings and labs run on the habitat itself")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        AUDIENCE

        Anyone who wants to know what a programming language IS —
        not by reading about interpreters, but by building one that
        runs a real multi-user operating system. The system you are
        reading this in (Nopales on Pricklypear) is the course's
        running example AND its lab bench: every reading is a spec
        chapter of the very interpreter you are using, served live
        at /book/course/cs-interpreters with runnable examples.

        OUTCOMES

        By the end you can, from source and from the habitat:
        1. Build a Lisp: reader, AST, evaluator, environments,
           closures, tail calls, lazy thunks ((delay)/(force)).
        2. Extend a language: add a special form or primitive to
           Nopales and prove it with a conformance test.
        3. Treat programs as data: read a function's AST at
           /binding/<name>, fork a branch, patch the AST, merge
           back, and diff two generations of a binding.

        HOW TO TAKE IT

        Read the chapters in course order (below). Examples in the
        habitat reader evaluate in YOUR session — run them as you
        read (habitat-first eval); `(book/verify)` re-checks every
        worked example. The labs are habitat objects, not
        worksheets: see the labs paragraph. The paper edition is
        the same chapters via
        `borge book print --root course/cs-interpreters.borg`.
   |)))
  (subsection readings
   (doc "Three chapters, in course order — each is the spec of a layer you then extend in the lab")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         1. borge/lisp-interpreter.borg — the language layer: the
            reader, the AST, the evaluator, environments, closures,
            tail calls, lazy thunks. Read `lisp/lib/eval.ml` as the
            primary source; it is the chapter's running code.
         2. borge/structural-editing.borg — programs as trees: one
            shared tree-op protocol (slurp, barf, wrap, splice,
            raise) over outlines AND code ASTs; you never type
            structure.
         3. borge/branching.borg — code as institutional data:
            branches as fork/merge over program text, AST diffing,
            inheritance of definitions on the one tree.

         Course order is deliberate: build the machine (1), then
         learn to operate on its programs as data (2), then see what
         a version-control system becomes when programs are data
         (3). The canonical book orders these chapters by system
         concern; the course re-orders them for the learner — a
         composition, never a copy.
   |)))
   (subsection labs
    (doc "The labs are habitat objects: routes on the running image, graded by whether the thing exists and works")
    (status implemented)
    (details
     (doc (|
          LAB 1 — extend the language (after reading 1). Add one
          special form or primitive to Nopales behind a conformance
          test in lisp/conformance/. Bench: /repl (try it), /code
          (read lisp/lib/), /binding/<name> (inspect what the reader
          made of your forms).

          LAB 2 — operate on a program as data (after readings 2-3).
          Fork a branch, patch a function's AST, merge back, and
          diff two generations of a binding. Bench: /branches,
          /bindings, /binding/<name>, and the /book chapter pages
          for branching and structural-editing.

          Report problems in log/issues (the habitat's own register)
          — issue hygiene is part of the coursework.
   |))))))	 
 (inline ../borge/lisp-interpreter.borg)
 (inline ../borge/structural-editing.borg)
 (inline ../borge/branching.borg))
